The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (prog->aux->func_cnt == 0) {
    ...
} else {
    for (i = 0; i < prog->aux->func_cnt; i++) {
        struct bpf_prog *subprog = prog->aux->func[i];
        ...
    }
}
```
Reason:
The logic only emitted ksymbol events for the main program when there were no subprograms (`prog->aux->func_cnt == 0`). When subprograms were present, it incorrectly assumed that the main program's symbol was not needed, leading to `prog->aux->func[0]->kallsyms` being uninitialized and missing in the output.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
perf_event_ksymbol(PERF_RECORD_KSYMBOL_TYPE_BPF,
                   (u64)(unsigned long)prog->bpf_func,
                   prog->jited_len, unregister,
                   prog->aux->ksym.name);

for (i = 1; i < prog->aux->func_cnt; i++) {
    struct bpf_prog *subprog = prog->aux->func[i];
    ...
}
```
Reason:
The fix ensures that the ksymbol event for the main program is always emitted, regardless of the presence of subprograms. This guarantees that the main program's symbol is recorded correctly, addressing the issue of the missing symbol in the output.

